body:
I'd like to give a short update about the changes that have gone into this API since this was discussed last (see also, our updated [explainer](https://github.com/WICG/html-in-canvas)):
* [`canvas.onpaint`](https://github.com/WICG/html-in-canvas?tab=readme-ov-file#3-the-onpaint-event) is a new event which fires late in the update-the-rendering steps after canvas children have changed. This is the ideal point to update the canvas with the rendering of elements. This new event avoids forced layouts, enables exposing the API to offscreen canvas in a worker thread, and has practical implementation benefits (including feasibility to implement in Gecko and WebKit as far as we can see, per our conversations with smfr and emilio) because it does not require an out-of-band paint step. The canvas children are painted during the document lifecycle and then the paint commands for those children can be drawn into the canvas in the `onpaint` event, and will appear in the current frame (i.e., no delay).
* Rendering of content under canvas is now restricted to be [privacy-preserving](https://github.com/WICG/html-in-canvas?tab=readme-ov-file#privacy-preserving-painting), limiting the new information exposed to javascript. This extends to the `onpaint` event, which only fires in response to changes of privacy-preserving painting.
* `drawElementImage` now returns a transform which can be used to [synchronize](https://github.com/WICG/html-in-canvas?tab=readme-ov-file#synchronization) how an element appears in canvas with its position in CSS/HTML (helpers are provided for WebGL/WebGPU too). This enables browser features such as hit testing, accessibility, and intersection observer.

### Basic Example
<img width="205" height="36" alt="a screenshot showing a form element with a blinking cursor" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/44fb3162-d179-4e0f-bc51-d1161f756513" />

```html
<canvas id="canvas" style="width: 200px; height: 200px;" layoutsubtree>
  <div id="form_element">
    name: <input>
  </div>
</canvas>

<script>
  const ctx = document.getElementById('canvas').getContext('2d');

  canvas.onpaint = () => {
    ctx.reset();
    let transform = ctx.drawElementImage(form_element, 0, 0);
    // Update the DOM position to match the drawn position.
    form_element.style.transform = transform.toString();
  };
</script>
```

Looking at the screen recording and this code:
```
  canvas.onpaint = () => {
    ctx.reset();
    let transform = ctx.drawElementImage(form_element, 0, 0);
    // Update the DOM position to match the drawn position.
    form_element.style.transform = transform.toString();
  };
```
makes me wonder: does `drawElementImage()` just draw an image/snapshot from the element `form_element` into the canvas? Or does it clone the DOM tree of `form_element` inside the canvas? The input element seems an alive element not a static drawing.

If `form_element` is drawn twice inside the canvas at two different positions, which `drawElementImage` return should be used to update the `form_element` position?
```
  canvas.onpaint = () => {
    ctx.reset();
    let transform1 = ctx.drawElementImage(form_element, 0, 0);
    let transform2 = ctx.drawElementImage(form_element, 0, 100);
    // Update the DOM position to match the drawn position.
    form_element.style.transform = transform1.toString(); // Or should it be transform2?
  };
```
